y Convention and Exhibition Centre, Sydney, New South Wales"] " (Hall 1 &amp; 2)"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/18850"} "23 from 23 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "Two single-elimination pools, with winner of each pool advanced to the final. All judoka losing to the finalists in each pool advanced to repêchage pools, with the winners of the repêchage pools earning bronze medals."]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "This class began without one highly considered " [:em {} "judoka"] ". Britain’s Debbie Allan had been the 1999 European Champion. But at the weigh-in she was found to be 400 gm. (14 oz.) overweight. The scales were noted to have been tampered with, so Allan was allowed to re-weigh. She had an hour to lose the weight. She cut her hair off, and stripped naked, but was still found to be 50 gm. (1.8 oz.) overweight and was disqualified. The British protested vociferously which almost led to the British team being disqualified as a group. The tournament would go according to form, however, with the finalists the same as those who met at the 1999 World Championship final. There " [:a {:href "/athletes/33321"} "Noriko Sugawara-Narazaki"] " (JPN) had defeated Cuba’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/33342"} "Legna Verdecia"] ". But this time Verdecia, who had been the 1993 World Champion, threw Sugawara-Narazaki with " [:em {} "seoi-otoshi"] " at 2:19 to win the gold medal. One of the bronze medalists was " [:a {:href "/athletes/33228"} "Kye Sun-Hui"] " of North Korea. She had won a gold medal in the 1996 extra-lightweight over Japan’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/33327"} "Ryoko Tamura"] ", which was a huge upset. But in the interim she had become one of the best female " [:em {} "judoka"] " in the world. Wallechinsky described the unusual support she received in Sydney, “Kye was cheered on by hundreds of " [:em {} "South"] " Korean fans, who, in the spirit of solidarity, sang traditional North Korean songs in her honor.”"] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/33342"} "Legna Verdecia"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/CUB"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/CUB.png"}] "CUB"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/33321"} "Noriko Sugawara-Narazaki"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/JPN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/JPN.png"}] "JPN"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "=3"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/33228"} "Kye Sun-Hui"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/PRK"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/PRK.png"}] "PRK"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Bronze"} "Bronze"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "=3"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/92253"} "Liu Yuxiang"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/CHN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/CHN.png"}] "CHN"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Bronze"} "Bronze"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "=5"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/92382"} "Deborah Gravenstijn"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NED"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NED.png"}] "NED"]] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "=5"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/92401"} "Ioana Dinea"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ROU"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertic